1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,200
In a basic rag pipeline, LLMs are only used for synthesis.

2
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:12,200
 The previous lesson showed you how to use LLMs to make a decision by picking a choice of different pipelines.

3
00:00:12,200 --> 00:00:15,120
 This is a simplified form of tool calling.

4
00:00:15,120 --> 00:00:19,920
 And in this lesson, we'll show you how to use an LLM to not only pick a function to execute,

5
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:23,000
 but also infer an argument to pass through the function.

6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:26,120
 All right, let's start coding.

7
00:00:26,120 --> 00:00:32,320
 One of the promises of LLMs is their ability to take actions and interact with their external environment.

8
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:37,320
 A necessary component to make this possible is a good interface for the LLMs to use.

9
00:00:37,320 --> 00:00:39,520
 We call this tool calling.

10
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:43,520
 In a basic rag pipeline, LLMs are only used for synthesis.

11
00:00:43,520 --> 00:00:48,320
 The previous lesson showed you how to use LLMs in a slightly more sophisticated manner

12
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:52,320
 by using it to pick the best query pipeline to answer the user query.

13
00:00:52,320 --> 00:00:55,320
 This is a simplified form of tool calling.

14
00:00:55,320 --> 00:01:00,120
 In this lesson, we'll show you how to use an LLM to not only pick a function to execute,

15
00:01:00,120 --> 00:01:03,520
 but also infer an argument to pass to the function.

16
00:01:03,520 --> 00:01:09,520
 This allows the LLM to figure out how to use a VectorDB instead of just consuming its outputs.

17
00:01:09,520 --> 00:01:15,320
 And the final result is that users are able to ask more questions and get back more precise results

18
00:01:15,320 --> 00:01:18,920
 than standard rag techniques through tool calling.

19
00:01:18,920 --> 00:01:20,520
 So let's get started.

20
00:01:20,520 --> 00:01:24,120
 Similar to before, we are first going to set up our opening IQ.

21
00:01:24,120 --> 00:01:27,720
 We are also going to set up and import our Nest async I/O module.

22
00:01:27,720 --> 00:01:32,120
 Next, we'll give you a basic overview and introduction to tool calling.

23
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:37,120
 We'll show you how to define a tool interface from a Python function.

24
00:01:37,120 --> 00:01:42,320
 And the LLM will automatically infer the parameters from the signature of the Python function

25
00:01:42,320 --> 00:01:44,920
 using LAMIT index abstractions.

26
00:01:44,920 --> 00:01:48,520
 To illustrate this, let's first define two toy calculator functions

27
00:01:48,520 --> 00:01:50,720
 to show you how tool calling works.

28
00:01:50,720 --> 00:01:55,520
 We'll define an add function and also a mystery function.

29
00:01:55,520 --> 00:01:59,720
 The core abstraction in LLM index is the function tool.

30
00:01:59,720 --> 00:02:03,920
 This function tool wraps any given Python function that you feed it.

31
00:02:03,920 --> 00:02:08,720
 And so we see that the function tool takes in both the add function defined here,

32
00:02:08,720 --> 00:02:13,720
 as well as a mystery function, which is just x plus y times x plus y.

33
00:02:13,720 --> 00:02:19,520
 You see that both add and mystery have type annotations for both the x and y variables,

34
00:02:19,520 --> 00:02:21,720
 as well as the doc string.

35
00:02:21,720 --> 00:02:24,120
 This is not just for stylistic purposes.

36
00:02:24,120 --> 00:02:29,920
 This is actually important because these things will be used as a prompt for the LLM.

37
00:02:29,920 --> 00:02:33,320
 Our function tools integrate natively with the function calling capabilities

38
00:02:33,320 --> 00:02:36,720
 of many LLM models, including OpenAI.

39
00:02:36,720 --> 00:02:40,720
 To pass the tools to an LLM, you have to import the LLM module

40
00:02:40,720 --> 00:02:45,720
 and then call predict and call.

41
00:02:45,720 --> 00:02:50,720
 This code snippet imports the OpenAI module explicitly.

42
00:02:50,720 --> 00:02:53,520
 And you see that the model is 3.5 turbo.

43
00:02:53,520 --> 00:02:57,920
 And then we call the predict and call function on top of the LLM.

44
00:02:57,920 --> 00:03:01,520
 What predict and call does is it takes in a set of tools,

45
00:03:01,520 --> 00:03:06,120
 as well as an input prompt string or a series of chat messages.

46
00:03:06,120 --> 00:03:09,720
 And then it's able to both make a decision of the tool to call,

47
00:03:09,720 --> 00:03:13,320
 as well as call the tool itself and get back the final response.

48
00:03:13,320 --> 00:03:16,720
 Here we see the intermediate steps calling function mystery

49
00:03:16,720 --> 00:03:19,520
 with the arguments x = 2, y = 9.

50
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:23,320
 So we see that at least I call the right tools and also infer the right parameters.

51
00:03:23,320 --> 00:03:25,320
 And the output is 121.

52
00:03:25,320 --> 00:03:27,120
 And 11 times 11 is 121.

53
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:28,520
 So we got back the right answer.

54
00:03:28,520 --> 00:03:33,920
 Note that this simple example effectively is an expanded version of the router.

55
00:03:33,920 --> 00:03:36,120
 Not only does the LLM pick the tool,

56
00:03:36,120 --> 00:03:38,920
 but it also decides what parameters to give to the tool.

57
00:03:38,920 --> 00:03:45,520
 Let's use this key concept to define a slightly more sophisticated agentic layer on top of vector search.

58
00:03:45,520 --> 00:03:48,120
 Not only can the LLM choose vector search,

59
00:03:48,120 --> 00:03:50,720
 we can also get it to infer metadata filters,

60
00:03:50,720 --> 00:03:57,120
 which is a structured list of tags that helps to return a more precise set of search results.

61
00:03:57,120 --> 00:04:00,320
 We'll use the same paper, metadata GPT, as before.

62
00:04:00,320 --> 00:04:04,520
 And this time let's pay attention to the nodes themselves or the chunks,

63
00:04:04,520 --> 00:04:08,520
 because we'll take a look at the actual metadata attached to these chunks.

64
00:04:08,520 --> 00:04:09,920
 Similar to the last lesson,

65
00:04:09,920 --> 00:04:18,520
 we'll use simple directory reader from LLM index to load in the parse representation of this PDF file.

66
00:04:18,520 --> 00:04:20,720
 Next, similar to the last lesson,

67
00:04:20,720 --> 00:04:29,320
 we'll use the sentence splitter to split these documents into a set of even chunks with a chunk size of 1024.

68
00:04:29,320 --> 00:04:31,720
 Each node here represents a chunk.

69
00:04:31,720 --> 00:04:35,920
 And let's actually take a look at the content of an example chunk.

70
00:04:35,920 --> 00:04:39,920
 So we'll take a look at the content of the very first chunk.

71
00:04:39,920 --> 00:04:44,320
 And we can do this from doing node.getContent.

72
00:04:44,320 --> 00:04:46,720
 We set metadata mode equals all,

73
00:04:46,720 --> 00:04:52,120
 which is a special setting to actually not just enable you to print out the content of the node itself,

74
00:04:52,120 --> 00:05:00,120
 but also the metadata attached to the document which is propagated to every node.

75
00:05:00,120 --> 00:05:08,120
 Here we see that once we print this out, we not only get back a parse representation of the front page of the paper,

76
00:05:08,120 --> 00:05:11,920
 we can also see the metadata attached at the very top.

77
00:05:11,920 --> 00:05:13,920
 So this includes a few things.

78
00:05:13,920 --> 00:05:16,720
 This includes page label equal to one.

79
00:05:16,720 --> 00:05:23,320
 This includes file name, metajpt.pdf, file type, file size, and created and end dates.

80
00:05:23,320 --> 00:05:25,920
 We'll pay special attention to the page labels,

81
00:05:25,920 --> 00:05:31,320
 because for instance, if we actually go in and try out a different node,

82
00:05:31,320 --> 00:05:34,920
 we see that we actually get back a different page number.

83
00:05:34,920 --> 00:05:39,720
 And so we actually add a page number annotation to every chunk.

84
00:05:39,720 --> 00:05:44,320
 Next, we'll define a vector store index over these nodes.

85
00:05:44,320 --> 00:05:49,920
 Similar to the last lesson, this will basically build a rag indexing pipeline over these nodes.

86
00:05:49,920 --> 00:05:54,520
 It will add an embedding for each node, and it will get back a query engine.

87
00:05:54,520 --> 00:06:00,120
 Differently from last time, we can actually try querying this rag pipeline via metadata filters,

88
00:06:00,120 --> 00:06:02,720
 just to show you how metadata filtering works.

89
00:06:02,720 --> 00:06:05,920
 So we import this object called metadata filters,

90
00:06:05,920 --> 00:06:11,120
 and then we simply specify a filter where the page label is equal to the value two,

91
00:06:11,120 --> 00:06:14,120
 in addition to topK equals two.

92
00:06:14,120 --> 00:06:16,320
 So once we define this as a query engine,

93
00:06:16,320 --> 00:06:20,520
 and we call it one or some high-level results of metajpt,

94
00:06:20,520 --> 00:06:23,320
 if we take a look at the source nodes,

95
00:06:23,320 --> 00:06:27,320
 once we run this and we call what are some high-level results of metajpt,

96
00:06:27,320 --> 00:06:29,120
 and we get back a response,

97
00:06:29,120 --> 00:06:33,320
 we'll first take a look at the response string,

98
00:06:33,320 --> 00:06:36,920
 which outlines the overall results of metajpt.

99
00:06:36,920 --> 00:06:44,520
 But crucially, we'll take a look at the page numbers.

100
00:06:44,520 --> 00:06:47,120
 And we see that as we iterate through the source nodes,

101
00:06:47,120 --> 00:06:50,520
 we can actually print out the metadata attached to these source nodes.

102
00:06:50,520 --> 00:06:53,320
 And we see that here the page label is equal to two.

103
00:06:53,320 --> 00:06:57,120
 And so we see that it's able to properly filter out the page numbers

104
00:06:57,120 --> 00:07:02,520
 to only restrict the search to the set of pages where the page label is equal to two.

105
00:07:02,520 --> 00:07:07,920
 The last section of this lesson will allow us to wrap this overall retrieval tool into a function.

106
00:07:07,920 --> 00:07:12,720
 This function takes in both a query string and page numbers as filters.

107
00:07:12,720 --> 00:07:17,720
 The LLM can actually then infer the page numbers to filter for the user query,

108
00:07:17,720 --> 00:07:21,520
 instead of actually having the user manually specify the metadata filters.

109
00:07:21,520 --> 00:07:25,920
 Just a quick note over here is that the metadata is not only limited to page numbers.

110
00:07:25,920 --> 00:07:29,120
 As you've seen, you can define whatever metadata you want,

111
00:07:29,120 --> 00:07:34,720
 like section IDs, headers, footers, or anything else through LLM index abstractions.

112
00:07:34,720 --> 00:07:39,920
 The ability to use many metadata filters is especially prominent in better models like Jupyter 4,

113
00:07:39,920 --> 00:07:41,920
 so we highly encourage you to check that out.

114
00:07:41,920 --> 00:07:45,320
 Here we'll define a Python function that encapsulates this.

115
00:07:45,320 --> 00:07:50,920
 We define a function called vector query, which takes in the query as well as page numbers.

116
00:07:50,920 --> 00:07:54,520
 This allows you to perform a vector search over an index,

117
00:07:54,520 --> 00:07:58,120
 along with specifying page numbers as a metadata filter.

118
00:07:58,120 --> 00:08:03,720
 At the very end, we see that we define a vector query tool equals function tool.fromDefaults.

119
00:08:03,720 --> 00:08:07,920
 So we pass in the vector query function into the vector query tool,

120
00:08:07,920 --> 00:08:12,120
 which allows us to then use it with a language model.

121
00:08:12,120 --> 00:08:17,120
 So let's try calling this tool with an LLM, specifically 3.5 turbo.

122
00:08:17,120 --> 00:08:22,320
 We'll find that the LLM is able to infer both the string as well as the metadata filters.

123
00:08:22,320 --> 00:08:26,720
 We do product and call on the vector query tool and ask the same question,

124
00:08:26,720 --> 00:08:31,720
 high-level results of MetaGBT as described on page 2.

125
00:08:31,720 --> 00:08:36,720
 Here we see that the LLM is able to formulate the right query, high-level results of MetaGBT,

126
00:08:36,720 --> 00:08:40,120
 as well as specify the page numbers, which is 2.

127
00:08:40,120 --> 00:08:44,720
 And we get back the correct answer.

128
00:08:44,720 --> 00:08:51,120
 Similarly as before, we can just quickly verify the source nodes.

129
00:08:51,120 --> 00:08:55,320
 And we see that the page label of the source nodes, there is one source node returned,

130
00:08:55,320 --> 00:08:58,120
 and it has a page label of 2.

131
00:08:58,120 --> 00:09:02,920
 Finally, we can bring in the summary tool from the router example in the first lesson,

132
00:09:02,920 --> 00:09:09,920
 and we can combine that with the vector tool to create this overall tool picking system.

133
00:09:09,920 --> 00:09:13,720
 So this code just sets up a summary index over the same set of nodes

134
00:09:13,720 --> 00:09:20,320
 and wraps this in a summary tool similar to lesson 1.

135
00:09:20,320 --> 00:09:22,320
 Now let's try tool calling again.

136
00:09:22,320 --> 00:09:25,920
 So the LLM has a slightly harder task of actually picking the right tool

137
00:09:25,920 --> 00:09:32,720
 in addition to inferring the function parameters.

138
00:09:32,720 --> 00:09:36,720
 We ask what are MetaGBT comparisons with ChatDev on page 8.

139
00:09:36,720 --> 00:09:41,520
 We see that it still calls a vector tool actually with page number equal to 8,

140
00:09:41,520 --> 00:09:45,520
 and it's able to still give back the right answer.

141
00:09:45,520 --> 00:09:50,920
 We can verify this by printing out the sources.

142
00:09:50,920 --> 00:09:53,720
 Lastly, we can ask the question, what is the summary of the paper,

143
00:09:53,720 --> 00:10:02,720
 to show that the LLM can still pick the summary tool when necessary.

144
00:10:02,720 --> 00:10:05,720
 And we see that it gives back the right response.

145
00:10:05,720 --> 00:10:07,720
 So that's it for lesson two, and in the next lesson,

146
00:10:07,720 --> 00:10:10,720
 we'll show you how to build a full agent over a document.

